// TrainFrame demo: two invented problems that show what a frame file can do.
// The language is described in docs/LANGUAGE.md.

@frame {demo}
title: A Tour of TrainFrame
class: Demo
folder: Examples
subtitle: Invented problems · not from any course
fact: Problems = 2
fact: Points = 10
fact: Language = v0.2
steps: per part
Each part opens on its first step. Try the next move on paper, then reveal the step to check it. Tags like F2 open the formula a step uses, right where you are.

@formula {F1} Exponential PDF
group: Distributions
$$ f_T(t) = \lambda e^{-\lambda t}, \qquad t \ge 0 $$
The waiting time until the first arrival, when arrivals come at rate $\lambda$.

@formula {F2} Exponential CDF
group: Distributions
$$ F_T(t) = 1 - e^{-\lambda t}, \qquad t \ge 0 $$

@formula {F3} Memoryless property
group: Distributions
$$ P[T > s + t \mid T > s] = P[T > t] $$

@formula {F4} RC step response
group: Circuits
$$ v_C(t) = V_s\left(1 - e^{-t/RC}\right), \qquad t \ge 0 $$
The capacitor charges toward $V_s$ with time constant $\tau = RC$.


// ---------------- Problem 1 ----------------

@problem {P1} Waiting for a bus
points: 6
short: Exponential wait
let lam = 0.2
let f(t) = if(t >= 0, lam * exp(-lam * t), 0)
let F(t) = if(t >= 0, 1 - exp(-lam * t), 0)

@given
Buses arrive at an average rate of $\lambda = 0.2$ per minute, so your wait $T$ is exponential ({{F1}}).

@plot Density of the wait
t: -1 to 20; ticks every 5; label "t (min)"
y: 0 to 0.22; ticks 0, 0.1, 0.2
curve f; legend "$f_T(t)$"

view Part a
shade f from 0 to 5
caption: Shaded area $= F_T(5) \approx {= F(5) : 3}$.

view Explore
slider b: 0 to 20; step 0.5; start 10; label "Wait up to $b$ minutes"
shade f from 0 to b
caption: $P[T \le b] = {= F(b) : 3}$

@part {a}
points: 3
Find the probability that you wait at most 5 minutes.

@step Pick the CDF {{F2}}
"At most 5 minutes" means $P[T \le 5]$, which is $F_T(5)$.

@step Plug in
$$ F_T(5) = 1 - e^{-0.2 \cdot 5} = 1 - e^{-1} $$

@answer
$$ P[T \le 5] = 1 - e^{-1} \approx 0.632 $$
check: integral(t = 0 to 5, f(t)) = 0.632

@part {b}
points: 3
You've already waited 5 minutes. Find the probability that you wait at least 5 more.

@step Use memorylessness {{F3}}
The time you've already spent doesn't change the wait still ahead:
$$ P[T > 10 \mid T > 5] = P[T > 5] $$

@step Use the CDF {{F2}}
$$ P[T > 5] = 1 - F_T(5) = e^{-1} $$

@answer
$$ P[T > 10 \mid T > 5] = e^{-1} \approx 0.368 $$
check: (1 - F(10)) / (1 - F(5)) = 0.368


// ---------------- Problem 2 ----------------

@problem {P2} Charging a capacitor
points: 4
short: RC charging
let Vs = 5
let R = 1000
let C = 0.001
let v(t) = if(t >= 0, Vs * (1 - exp(-t / (R * C))), 0)

@given
A $1\,\text{k}\Omega$ resistor and a $1\,\text{mF}$ capacitor are switched onto a $5\,\text{V}$ source at $t = 0$. The capacitor starts uncharged.

@part {a}
points: 4
How long does the capacitor take to reach 4 V?

@step Find the time constant {{F4}}
$$ \tau = RC = (1000)(0.001) = 1\ \text{s} $$

@step Solve for t
$$ 4 = 5\left(1 - e^{-t}\right) \;\Longrightarrow\; e^{-t} = 0.2 \;\Longrightarrow\; t = \ln 5 $$

@answer
$$ t = \ln 5 \approx 1.609\ \text{s} $$
check: v(ln(5)) = 4

@plot Check it · the charging curve
place: after steps
t: -0.5 to 5; ticks every 1; label "t (s)"
y: 0 to 5.5; ticks 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5; label "$v_C$ (V)"
curve v; legend "$v_C(t)$"
mark y = 4; dashed; label "4 V"
mark x = ln(5); label "t = ln 5"
caption: The curve crosses 4 V at $t = \ln 5 \approx {= ln(5) : 3}$ s, a little over one time constant.

@checklist Before you submit
- Every probability is between 0 and 1.
- Every time has its unit.
